    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nominoe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nominoe.jpg" alt="Nominoe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nom%C3%A9no%C3%AB_(Tenniel).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Tenniel / Joseph Swain, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brittany, the Armorican peninsula settled by migrants from south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the fifth and sixth centuries, had formed a kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salomon-of-brittany">Salomon</a> in the ninth century, when its rulers fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> and won the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. After Salomon’s murder in 874 and the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-i-king-of-brittany">Alan the Great</a> around 907, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids intensified, and in 919 a Norse army overran the country; monks fled with their relics and many nobles took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Alan, grandson of Alan the Great and later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-ii-duke-of-brittany">Barbetorte</a> or Twisted Beard, grew up at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>. In 936 he returned with English support, recovered Nantes, and completed the reconquest with a victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trans">Trans</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dol-de-bretagne">Dol</a>, traditionally dated to 1 August 939. Alan II ruled as duke rather than king until his death in 952, with Nantes as his base. His successors were challenged by the counts of Rennes, whose Conan I claimed the ducal title and died fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-nerra">Fulk Nerra of Anjou</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-conquereuil">Battle of Conquereuil</a> in 992. The frontier marches with Anjou and Normandy and the rivalry between Nantes and Rennes shaped the duchy’s early politics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rennes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rennes.jpg" alt="Rennes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_de_la_Mairie,_Rennes,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brittany was divided by language: Breton was spoken in the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-brittany">Lower Brittany</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-language">Gallo</a> and French prevailed in the east around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, where most towns and the ducal administration were concentrated. The great majority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> were peasants farming small fields in hedged bocage country, and in Lower Brittany many held land under the <em>domaine congéable</em>, a distinctive tenure in which the tenant owned the buildings and the lord the soil. The church was organised in nine dioceses, and local saints’ cults and pilgrimages marked religious life; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-of-kermartin">Ivo of Kermartin</a>, a priest and judge who died in 1303, was canonised in 1347 as Saint Yves, later patron of lawyers. The economy leaned towards the sea. Salt from the marshes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bourgneuf">Bay of Bourgneuf</a>, linen and hemp canvas from inland weaving districts, and fish were exported, and from the fourteenth century Breton ships carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> wine, salt and cloth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. The dukes profited by selling sea passes (<em>brefs</em>) to shipmasters. By the fifteenth century Nantes, Rennes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> were prosperous ducal towns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-nantes">University of Nantes</a> in 1460.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings.jpg" alt="Battle of Hastings" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene57_Harold_death.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy’s armies were raised from its numerous and often modest nobility, town militias and coastal levies. Breton knights served widely abroad: a large Breton contingent fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066, and its leaders, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-rufus">Alan the Red</a>, received great estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, including the honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia">Richmond</a>. Brittany’s position between the Angevin and Capetian worlds drew it into their wars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> forced Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conan-iv-duke-of-brittany">Conan IV</a> to surrender power in 1166. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-breton-succession">War of the Breton Succession</a> (1341–1364) made Brittany a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the Hundred Years’ War, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a> and England supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-montfort">John of Montfort</a>. It produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/combat-of-the-thirty">Combat of the Thirty</a> in 1351 and ended at the Battle of Auray on 29 September 1364, where Charles was killed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-du-guesclin">Bertrand du Guesclin</a> captured. The dukes guarded the march with Anjou and Poitou with fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clisson">Clisson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fougeres">Fougères</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateaubriant">Châteaubriant</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-iii-duke-of-brittany">Arthur de Richemont</a>, constable of France and later Duke Arthur III, helped reform the French royal army and shared in the victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-formigny">Battle of Formigny</a> in 1450. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>’s army, reinforced by English, German and Castilian troops, was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille">Louis de La Trémoille</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-aubin-du-cormier">Battle of Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier</a> on 28 July 1488.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Arthur I, Duke of Brittany" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany">Arthur I, Duke of Brittany</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artur_of_Brittany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes held Brittany as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the French crown, but the nature of their homage was disputed: the Montfort dukes insisted that they owed only simple homage, not liege homage, and styled themselves dukes “by the grace of God”. Henry II’s son Geoffrey ruled through his marriage to the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a>, and after their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-i-duke-of-brittany">Arthur of Brittany</a> disappeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>’s custody around 1203, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> gave the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-i-duke-of-brittany">Peter Mauclerc</a> of the house of Dreux in 1213. Peter and his successors strengthened ducal administration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a> made John II a peer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1297. Under the Montfort dukes from 1364 Brittany developed institutions resembling those of a sovereign state: a ducal council, a chancery, a chamber of accounts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, a high court known as the Parlement or Grands Jours, regular taxation through the <em>fouage</em> hearth tax, and Estates representing clergy, nobility and towns. The dukes minted their own gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, were crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes-cathedral">Rennes Cathedral</a> and upheld a written customary law, the <em>Très ancienne coutume de Bretagne</em>, compiled in the early fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-v-duke-of-brittany">John V</a> kept the duchy largely neutral during the later Hundred Years’ War.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nantes-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nantes-cathedral.jpg" alt="Nantes Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nantes-cathedral">Nantes Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cath%C3%A9drale_Saint-Pierre_de_Nantes_-_fa%C3%A7ade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eusebius, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Brittany preserved the only Celtic language spoken on the European mainland and a regional identity that survived its absorption into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Breton storytellers helped carry the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matter-of-britain">Matter of Britain</a> into French literature: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-de-france">Marie de France</a> presented her twelfth-century lais as Breton tales, and the forest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/broceliande">Brocéliande</a> became a setting of Arthurian romance. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicon-breton-dictionary">Catholicon</a></em> of Jehan Lagadeuc, compiled in 1464 and printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> in 1499, is the first Breton dictionary and one of the earliest dictionaries of French. Breton nobles and soldiers shaped English and French history alike, from the Breton lords of post-Conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-du-guesclin">Bertrand du Guesclin</a>, constable of France under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olivier-de-clisson">Olivier de Clisson</a>. The ducal court sponsored histories of Brittany by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-baud">Pierre Le Baud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alain-bouchart">Alain Bouchart</a> that promoted an image of ancient independence, and art such as the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes-cathedral">Nantes Cathedral</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-colombe">Michel Colombe</a> and the <em>Grandes Heures</em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a> illuminated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bourdichon">Jean Bourdichon</a>. The privileges guaranteed in 1532, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estates-of-brittany">Estates of Brittany</a> and the Parlement created in 1554 and fixed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> in 1561 kept a strong provincial identity alive until the French Revolution, and Anne of Brittany remains a symbol of the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal.jpg" alt="League of the Public Weal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal">League of the Public Weal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerre_du_Bien_public.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circle of Étienne Colaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy’s independence ended through war and marriage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, who had no son, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal">League of the Public Weal</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> in 1465 and later sheltered princes in revolt against the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-france">Anne of Beaujeu</a>, giving the crown reason to intervene. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-aubin-du-cormier">Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier</a> in July 1488, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-le-verger">Treaty of Le Verger</a> forbade his daughters to marry without the king’s consent, and Francis died weeks later, leaving the eleven-year-old Anne as duchess. Her proxy marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Austria</a> in 1490 brought French armies back; besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, Anne agreed to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a>, and the wedding took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langeais">Langeais</a> in December 1491. When Charles died in 1498 she resumed rule of her duchy and married his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII</a> in 1499 under a contract meant to keep Brittany separate. Anne died in 1514, and her daughter Claude, wife of Francis I, left the duchy to their son the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-iii-duke-of-brittany">Dauphin Francis</a>. In 1532 the Estates meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> asked for perpetual union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, confirmed by royal edict that year, which preserved Breton privileges and consent to taxation. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Dauphin Henry</a>, duke since his brother’s death in 1536, became Henry II in 1547, the ducal title merged with the crown.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of Brittany shows how a Celtic-speaking frontier region built near-sovereign institutions of its own under the Montfort dukes, and how dynastic marriage as much as conquest brought it into the French kingdom while preserving privileges and an identity that outlasted the union by centuries.</em></p>

